{
  "id": "compound-angles",
  "title": "Trigonometry: Compound and Double Angles",
  "term": 2,
  "summary": "The compound-angle and double-angle identities, using them to simplify expressions, prove identities and solve equations.",
  "notes": [
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Compound-angle identities" },
    { "type": "formula", "latex": "\\cos(\\alpha - \\beta) = \\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta + \\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta \\qquad \\cos(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\cos\\alpha\\cos\\beta - \\sin\\alpha\\sin\\beta" },
    { "type": "formula", "latex": "\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta) = \\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta + \\cos\\alpha\\sin\\beta \\qquad \\sin(\\alpha - \\beta) = \\sin\\alpha\\cos\\beta - \\cos\\alpha\\sin\\beta" },
    { "type": "callout", "label": "Common mistake", "text": "$\\sin(\\alpha + \\beta) \\neq \\sin\\alpha + \\sin\\beta$. For example, $\\sin(30^\\circ + 60^\\circ) = \\sin 90^\\circ = 1$, but $\\sin 30^\\circ + \\sin 60^\\circ \\approx 1{,}37$." },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 1", "problem": "Without a calculator, find $\\cos 15^\\circ$.", "steps": [
      "$\\cos 15^\\circ = \\cos(45^\\circ - 30^\\circ) = \\cos 45^\\circ\\cos 30^\\circ + \\sin 45^\\circ\\sin 30^\\circ$",
      "$= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2} + \\dfrac{\\sqrt{2}}{2} \\cdot \\dfrac{1}{2}$",
      "$= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}$"
    ], "answer": "$\\frac{\\sqrt{6} + \\sqrt{2}}{4}$" },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 2: recognising the expansion", "problem": "Simplify $\\sin 70^\\circ\\cos 10^\\circ - \\cos 70^\\circ\\sin 10^\\circ$", "steps": ["This is $\\sin(70^\\circ - 10^\\circ) = \\sin 60^\\circ$", "$= \\dfrac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$"], "answer": "$\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$" },
    { "type": "heading", "text": "Double-angle identities" },
    { "type": "formula", "latex": "\\sin 2\\alpha = 2\\sin\\alpha\\cos\\alpha" },
    { "type": "formula", "latex": "\\cos 2\\alpha = \\cos^2\\alpha - \\sin^2\\alpha = 2\\cos^2\\alpha - 1 = 1 - 2\\sin^2\\alpha" },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 3", "problem": "If $\\sin\\theta = \\frac{3}{5}$ and $\\theta$ is acute, find $\\sin 2\\theta$ and $\\cos 2\\theta$.", "steps": [
      "Draw a triangle: opposite 3, hypotenuse 5, adjacent 4, so $\\cos\\theta = \\frac{4}{5}$.",
      "$\\sin 2\\theta = 2 \\cdot \\frac{3}{5} \\cdot \\frac{4}{5} = \\frac{24}{25}$",
      "$\\cos 2\\theta = 1 - 2\\left(\\frac{3}{5}\\right)^2 = \\frac{7}{25}$"
    ], "answer": "$\\frac{24}{25}$ and $\\frac{7}{25}$" },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 4: proving an identity", "problem": "Prove that $\\dfrac{\\sin 2x}{1 + \\cos 2x} = \\tan x$", "steps": [
      "LHS $= \\dfrac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{1 + 2\\cos^2 x - 1}$",
      "$= \\dfrac{2\\sin x\\cos x}{2\\cos^2 x} = \\dfrac{\\sin x}{\\cos x} = \\tan x =$ RHS"
    ], "answer": "LHS = RHS" },
    { "type": "callout", "label": "Exam tip", "text": "Choose the form of $\\cos 2\\alpha$ that cancels something: use $2\\cos^2\\alpha - 1$ next to a '$+1$', and $1 - 2\\sin^2\\alpha$ next to a '$1 -$'." },
    { "type": "example", "title": "Worked example 5: equation", "problem": "Find the general solution of $\\sin 2x = \\cos x$.", "steps": [
      "$2\\sin x\\cos x - \\cos x = 0$, so $\\cos x(2\\sin x - 1) = 0$",
      "$\\cos x = 0$: $x = 90^\\circ + k \\cdot 180^\\circ$",
      "$\\sin x = \\frac{1}{2}$: $x = 30^\\circ + k \\cdot 360^\\circ$ or $x = 150^\\circ + k \\cdot 360^\\circ$, $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$"
    ], "answer": "$x = 90^\\circ + k \\cdot 180^\\circ$; $30^\\circ + k \\cdot 360^\\circ$; $150^\\circ + k \\cdot 360^\\circ$" }
  ],
